45493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575515 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575515 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186575128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575515 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e425688d-cefc-532f-960d-cb1285b1d995" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575520 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186574993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575520 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186575228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575520 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186575237 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575520 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186575256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575520 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186575294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575520 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575520 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575520 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186575228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575520 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f0f7f6bf-8e15-5716-bb8d-0bc39135a77f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575530 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186575515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575530 #edb/ref 1069 "q-175389" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575530 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575530 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following predicate:\n\n$\\,$ $P(x,y):$ $-x+y\\ge -1,$\n\nwhere the universal set is ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following shaded regions represents the truth set of the predicate?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575530 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "11e39500-6de6-5573-ad9b-383cb406fe12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575530 #edb/ref 1074 "The *truth set* of a predicate $P({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})$ is the set of $n$-tuples in its universal set $U={U}_{1}\\times {U}_{2}\\times ⋯\\times {U}_{n}$ such that substituting these tuples for the variables makes the predicate a true statement:\n\n$$\nT=\\{({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\in U\\,|\\,P({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\}\n$$\n\nThe universal set for our predicate is $U={R}^{2}.$\n\nWe need to find $(x,y)\\in U$ such that the following is true:\n\n$\\,$ $-x+y\\ge -1$\n\nNow, we convert the inequality into the slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+y & \\ge -1 \\\\ y & \\ge x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's sketch the truth set $T$ of $P(x,y):$\n\n![](images/8d/8dabb7d13ef6e46cc31a2613bf7d62a5163db96630e9a855e84b9335e3be4855.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575534 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186575505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575534 #edb/ref 1069 "q-175353" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575534 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575534 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the truth set of the following predicate:\n\n$\\,$ $x$ is divisible by $3,$\n\nwhere the universal set is the set of integers from $5$ to $20$ inclusive." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575534 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "260f8856-8d02-57c2-89b0-9f4ad3929290" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575534 #edb/ref 1074 "The *truth set* $T$ of a predicate $P(x)$ is the set of elements in its universal set $U$ such that substituting these elements for the variable makes the predicate a true statement:\n\n$$\nT=\\{x\\in U\\,|\\,P(x)\\}\n$$\n\nThe universal set for our predicate is\n\n$$\nU=\\{5,6,…,20\\}.\n$$\n\nWe need to find $x\\in U$ such that the following is true:\n\n$\\,$ $x$ is divisible by $3.$\n\nThe following numbers give the elements of our truth set:\n\n- $x=6$ since $6$ is divisible by $3,$\n\n- $x=9$ since $9$ is divisible by $3,$\n\n- $x=12$ since $12$ is divisible by $3,$\n\n- $x=15$ since $15$ is divisible by $3,$\n\n- $x=18$ since $18$ is divisible by $3.$\n\nTherefore, the truth set of our predicate is\n\n$$\nT=\\{6,9,12,15,18\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575535 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186575439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575535 #edb/ref 1069 "q-175346" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575535 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575535 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following are mathematical predicates?\n\nI. ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ is a negative number.\n\nII. $q$ is a multiple of $3.$\n\nIII. $p$ is an irrational number." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575535 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "29e9adcf-4a5b-51ac-835b-f4a444c16f67" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575535 #edb/ref 1074 "A *predicate* of the variables ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is a sentence that contains the variables defined on some *universal set*, and has the property that when we substitute concrete values for all the variables into the predicate, the result becomes a statement that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our options. \n\n- Option I is a predicate of two variables. Substituting a concrete pair of values $(x,y)$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option II is a predicate of one variable. Substituting a concrete value for $q$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option III is a predicate of one variable. Substituting a concrete value for $p$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575553 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186575497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575553 #edb/ref 1069 "q-174455" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575553 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575553 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the truth set of the following predicate:\n\n$\\,$ $P(x):$ ${x}^{2}\\ge -1$\n\nwhere the universal set is $N.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575553 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "63a5ac6a-aab7-51ca-954b-e590d679441c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575553 #edb/ref 1074 "The *truth set* of a predicate $P(x)$ is the set of elements in its universal set $U$ such that substituting these elements for the variable makes the predicate a true statement:\n\n$$\nT=\\{x\\in U\\,|\\,P(x)\\}\n$$\n\nThe universal set for our predicate is $U=N.$\n\nWe need to find $x\\in U$ such that the following is true:\n\n$\\,$ ${x}^{2}\\ge -1$\n\nWe know that the square of any real number is greater than or equal to zero.\n\nSo, the inequality is true for every real number. In particular, it's true for every $x\\in N.$ \n\nTherefore, the truth set of our predicate is $T=N.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575564 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186575485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575564 #edb/ref 1069 "q-175387" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575564 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575564 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following predicate:\n\n$\\,$ $P(x,y):$ $x+y<3,$\n\nwhere the universal set is ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following shaded regions represents the truth set of the predicate?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575564 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "78dba0d1-f872-5b82-8f2b-f5d37f8cf2ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575564 #edb/ref 1074 "The *truth set* of a predicate $P({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})$ is the set of $n$-tuples in its universal set $U={U}_{1}\\times {U}_{2}\\times ⋯\\times {U}_{n}$ such that substituting these tuples for the variables makes the predicate a true statement:\n\n$$\nT=\\{({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\in U\\,|\\,P({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\}\n$$\n\nThe universal set for our predicate is $U={R}^{2}.$\n\nWe need to find $(x,y)\\in U$ such that the following is true:\n\n$\\,$ $x+y<3$\n\nNow, we convert the inequality into the slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & <3 \\\\ y & <-x+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's sketch the truth set $T$ of $P(x,y):$\n\n![](images/bd/bde3d6ee1e58d33088ffc49913e1cd8a299f0ea91cdf4024fd0b63a598ebf456.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575580 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186575476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575580 #edb/ref 1069 "q-175354" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575580 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575580 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following are mathematical predicates?\n\nI. $8$ divided by $t.$\n\nII. $y$ multiplied by $z.$\n\nIII. $p$ is a composite number." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575580 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9d2ae0ef-2e2b-52c8-a273-c086772b799e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575580 #edb/ref 1074 "A *predicate* of the variables ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is a sentence that contains the variables defined on some *universal set*, and has the property that when we substitute concrete values for all the variables into the predicate, the result becomes a statement that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our options. \n\n- Option I is *not* a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $t$ into the sentence, we do not get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option II is *not* a predicate. Substituting a concrete pair of values $(y,z)$ into the sentence, we do not get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option III is a predicate of one variable. Substituting a concrete value for $p$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575588 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186575504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575588 #edb/ref 1069 "q-175364" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575588 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575588 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the truth set of the following predicate:\n\n$\\,$ $P(x):$ ${x}^{2}-8=2x$\n\nwhere the universal set is $R.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575588 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b1aeeb90-b252-58b0-bd1f-5b12f471387e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575588 #edb/ref 1074 "The *truth set* of a predicate $P(x)$ is the set of elements in its universal set $U$ such that substituting these elements for the variable makes the predicate a true statement:\n\n$$\nT=\\{x\\in U\\,|\\,P(x)\\}\n$$\n\nThe universal set for our predicate is $U=R.$\n\nWe need to find $x\\in U$ such that the following is true:\n\n$\\,$ ${x}^{2}-8=2x$\n\nNow, we solve the above equation by factoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-8 & =2x \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x-8 & =0 \\\\ (x-4)(x+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-2\\in R$ and $x=4\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the truth set of our predicate is $T=\\{-2,4\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575590 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186575484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575590 #edb/ref 1069 "q-175352" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575590 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575590 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following are mathematical predicates?\n\nI. $q$ plus $3.$\n\nII. $2$ is greater than $x.$\n\nIII. $a$ is a divisor of $b.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575590 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b5cdaff3-81ba-58ae-a27f-1dea94cd990d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575590 #edb/ref 1074 "A *predicate* of the variables ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is a sentence that contains the variables defined on some *universal set*, and has the property that when we substitute concrete values for all the variables into the predicate, the result becomes a statement that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our options. \n\n- Option I is *not* a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $q$ into the sentence, we do not get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option II is a predicate of one variable. Substituting a concrete value for $x$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option III is a predicate of two variables. Substituting a concrete pair of values $(a,b)$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575593 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186575424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575593 #edb/ref 1069 "q-175358" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575593 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575593 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the truth set of the following predicate:\n\n$\\,$ $P(x):$ $4x-3\\ge 9,$\n\nwhere the universal set is $R.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575593 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b91cf75b-843d-5d15-ac06-6485f918c5af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575593 #edb/ref 1074 "The *truth set* of a predicate $P(x)$ is the set of elements in its universal set $U$ such that substituting these elements for the variable makes the predicate a true statement:\n\n$$\nT=\\{x\\in U\\,|\\,P(x)\\}\n$$\n\nWe need to find $x\\in U$ such that the following is true:\n\n$\\,$ $4x-3\\ge 9$\n\nThe universal set for our predicate is $U=R.$\n\nNow, we need to solve the above inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-3\\ge 9 \\\\ 4x\\ge 12 \\\\ x\\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the truth set of our predicate is\n\n$$\nT=[3,\\infty ).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575599 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186575520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575599 #edb/ref 1069 "q-174401" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575599 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575599 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following predicate:\n\n$\\,$ $P(x,y):$ ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}<4,$\n\nwhere the universal set is ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following shaded regions represents the truth set of the predicate?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575599 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ca53c000-4fe4-56e9-a6ee-1afd2eed227d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575599 #edb/ref 1074 "The *truth set* of a predicate $P({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})$ is the set of $n$-tuples in its universal set $U={U}_{1}\\times {U}_{2}\\times ⋯\\times {U}_{n}$ such that substituting these tuples for the variables makes the predicate a true statement:\n\n$$\nT=\\{({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\in U\\,|\\,P({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\}\n$$\n\nThe universal set for our predicate is $U={R}^{2}.$\n\nWe need to find $(x,y)\\in U$ such that the following is true:\n\n$\\,$ ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}<4$\n\nLet's first rearrange our inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}<4 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{4}<1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}<1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that this represents an ellipse centered at $O$ with semi-axes $2$ and $1.$\n\nFinally, let's sketch the truth set $T$ of $P(x,y):$\n\n![](images/fd/fdb0a299dd6ee0bd58ceb1a74c9423bf5cac81857b0538dcda7f0a4cba9a1108.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575605 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186575432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575605 #edb/ref 1069 "q-175383" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575605 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575605 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the truth set of the following predicate:\n\n$\\,$ $a+b$ is an odd number,\n\nwhere the universal set is $\\{(a,b)\\,|\\,a=2,3,\\,b=5,8\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575605 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d193d524-b905-5ac6-8d71-b915f99a8936" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575605 #edb/ref 1074 "The *truth set* of a predicate $P({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})$ is the set of $n$-tuples in its universal set $U={U}_{1}\\times {U}_{2}\\times ⋯\\times {U}_{n}$ such that substituting these tuples for the variables makes the predicate a true statement:\n\n$$\nT=\\{({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\in U\\,|\\,P({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\}\n$$\n\nThe universal set for our predicate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =\\{2,3\\}\\times \\{5,8\\} \\\\  & =\\{(2,5),(2,8),(3,5),(3,8)\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to find $(a,b)\\in U$ such that the following is true:\n\n$\\,$ $a+b$ is an odd number\n\nThe following pairs give the elements of our truth set:\n\n- $(2,5)$ since $2+5=7$ and $7$ is an odd number,\n\n- $(3,8)$ since $3+8=11$ and $11$ is an odd number.\n\nTherefore, the truth set of our predicate is\n\n$$\nT=\\{(2,5),(3,8)\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
